## Organizations (multi-tenancy)

This service is multi-tenant: every authenticated user can create **organizations** and
each `(organization, user)` pair is a **membership** carrying a `role`. The `/orgs`
router is the example surface:

```text
POST   /orgs                          create an org (caller becomes its owner)
GET    /orgs                          list the orgs the caller belongs to
GET    /orgs/{org_id}/members         list members (caller must belong to the org)
POST   /orgs/{org_id}/members         add a member
DELETE /orgs/{org_id}/members/{user}  remove a member
```

The `Organization` and `Membership` models live in `app.tenancy.models`; the per-membership
`role` is a free string (`owner` / `admin` / `member` by convention) ready to be enforced by
a permissions layer.

{% if include_rls %}
### Tenant isolation (Postgres RLS)

A second isolation layer backstops the app-level org scoping: every tenant **data** table has a
Row-Level Security policy, and the per-tenant request path connects as an RLS-bound role with the
`app.current_tenant` GUC set from `/orgs/{org_id}`. So a query that forgets its `WHERE
organization_id` still can't read another tenant's rows. Postgres-only — a no-op on SQLite.

**Two roles** keep it safe (RLS is `FORCE`d, so the table owner is bound too):

- `DATABASE_URL` → a least-privilege, **RLS-bound** role — the per-tenant request path.
- `DATABASE_URL_PRIVILEGED` → a **`BYPASSRLS`** role for flows that legitimately span tenants:
  API-key auth, the admin panel, inbound provider webhooks, and background workers — including the
  outbox relay, which reads every tenant's endpoints. Leave it unset in dev and both use
  `DATABASE_URL` (`app.tenancy.rls` has the details). **In production point it at a real `BYPASSRLS`
  role:** if it resolves to the RLS-bound role the relay reads zero endpoints and silently drops
  every webhook (the relay has no tenant in context, so the policy hides all rows).

Provision once (as the DB owner; run migrations as the owner, the app connects as these roles):

```sql
CREATE ROLE app_rls     LOGIN PASSWORD '...';                -- DATABASE_URL
CREATE ROLE app_trusted LOGIN PASSWORD '...' BYPASSRLS;      -- DATABASE_URL_PRIVILEGED
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO app_rls, app_trusted;
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO app_rls, app_trusted;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public
  GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON TABLES TO app_rls, app_trusted;
```

Use **session-mode** pooling (PgBouncer `session`, not `transaction`) so the session-level GUC
stays attached to the backend connection for the whole request.
{% endif %}
{% if include_rbac %}
### Roles & permissions (RBAC)

The `app.rbac` package turns that `role` into enforcement. `ROLE_RANK` orders the roles
(`owner > admin > member` — extend by adding one) and `SCOPES_BY_ROLE` maps each role to
permission scopes. Two FastAPI dependency factories guard routes:

- `require_role("admin")` — the caller must hold at least that role in the org, else `403`.
- `require_scope("members:write")` — the caller's role must grant that scope, else `403`.

They ship applied as the example: adding a member requires the `admin` role and removing a
member requires the `members:write` scope, so a plain `member` is denied both.

