## Email & notifications (provider-agnostic)

Email goes through a **hexagonal port**: `app.email.ports.EmailPort` is the app's-needs contract
(`send(EmailMessage)` over a normalized, vendor-neutral message); `app.email.adapters` holds the
**SMTP** (aiosmtplib) and **Amazon SES** (aioboto3) implementations plus a credential-free
console/suppress backend; the active one is selected by `EMAIL_PROVIDER` (default
`{{ email_provider }}`). Nothing in the app imports a vendor SDK — it speaks only the port and the
normalized `EmailMessage`. Bodies are rendered from jinja2 templates in `app.email.templates` by a
typed helper (`app.email.send.send_template`), so callers pass a template name + context, never raw
HTML. `get_email_provider()` selects the adapter exactly like `get_payments_provider`.

```text
EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp        SMTP (Zoho / Microsoft 365 / any host) — the default
EMAIL_PROVIDER=ses         Amazon SES over the AWS API (pairs with S3-compatible storage)
EMAIL_SUPPRESS_SEND=true   dev/test: capture to the console backend, send nothing
```

Nothing sends until the active provider is configured (no `SMTP_HOST` / no SES keys → the send is
logged and skipped), so a fresh dev environment never blocks on email. Configure the active
provider's credentials per environment — never hard-coded:
- **SMTP** (`EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp`): `SMTP_HOST`, `SMTP_PORT` (587), `SMTP_USERNAME`,
  `SMTP_PASSWORD`, `SMTP_START_TLS` / `SMTP_USE_TLS`, plus `EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS`.
- **SES** (`EMAIL_PROVIDER=ses`): `SES_REGION`, `SES_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `SES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`
  (or leave the keys unset to use an instance IAM role), plus `EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS`.

**Adding an email provider** = implement `EmailPort` in `app.email.adapters`, register it in
`app.email.provider`, and add its key to the `email_provider` choices — see AGENTS.md.
