## Background jobs & scheduler (arq)

Long-running or fire-and-forget work runs **out of band** on an
[arq](https://arq-docs.helpmanual.io/) worker backed by Redis, so the request never waits on it.
Call sites enqueue through a thin seam and never import arq:

```python
from app.jobs.queue import enqueue

await enqueue("example_task", 21)   # the worker runs example_task(ctx, 21)
```

- **Worker:** `just worker` (runs `arq app.jobs.worker.WorkerSettings`) — needs `REDIS_URL`; run
  it as a second process alongside `just run`.
- **Tasks** live in `app.jobs.tasks`; register a new one in `WorkerSettings.functions`.
- **Recurring jobs** use arq's built-in **cron** (no extra dependency): the example `heartbeat`
  runs every 5 minutes — see `WorkerSettings.cron_jobs`.

arq is used **directly**, not behind a runtime port — the queue/scheduler is an infrastructure
choice, not a swappable vendor, so abstracting it would be over-engineering.
{% if include_email %}When the email capability is on, the user manager's **welcome** and
**password-reset** emails are **enqueued** (`send_email_task`) instead of sent inline, so a slow
or failing email provider can't delay or break registration / reset requests.{% endif %}

{% endif %}
